    "turn_text": "Well, it's it's shocking to me, too, because I think the president has had a very strong economic record. But I would say two things. One, we had inflation for three years. Now that inflation was caused because of COVID, because of pandemic shocks, because of spending that started in Donald Trump's administration and was necessary in continuing Biden's administration. We don't talk about the fact that most of that spending, a lot of it, was under Trump and I defend it because we needed to give people checks. We needed to make sure that the unemployment rate came down and President Biden fortunately also made sure that we weren't going to have massive unemployment. So the, but the reality is that for three years, prices went up and we don't have this inflation. So the price of groceries is high for folks. The price of a rent is high for folks and wages has started to go up over the last six months, but they have not kept up with all the accumulation of costs going up. And that's why I think you have people. anxious. And then you have the bigger issue of people anxious about the American dream. You know, my district, Silicon Valley, $10 trillion a market value and video hit $2 trillion the other day. The world seems the oyster for a lot of young folks in other parts of this country that face the industrialization, that face economic loss. Young people say, I can't buy a house. I don't know if I'm going to have a good paying job. This American dream seems vanishing. So the president, in my view, has to speak to the fact that there's still a lot of economic anxiety there. We have a lot more work to do. He understands the future economy where we're going, and he understands how we're going to give people a shot. In some ways, like FDR did in 1936. You know, FDR did a lot, but he spent 80% of his speech in 36 talking about how the country still needed a lot of things changed. And then he, President Biden, should contrast it with Donald Trump.",
